# Documentation Standards Rules
# Loaded when documentation standards are required
# Auto-generated by Dynamic Cursor Rules System

## DOCUMENTATION STANDARDS (COMPREHENSIVE)

### Documentation Levels (Fractal)
All systems have L0-L4 documentation:
- **L0:** 100 words (executive summary)
- **L1:** 500 words (overview)
- **L2:** 2,000 words (architecture)
- **L3:** 10,000 words (implementation)
- **L4:** 15,000+ words (complete)

### Documentation Structure
- Clear, logical organization
- Consistent formatting
- Proper headings and sections
- Table of contents for long documents
- Cross-references and links

### Content Standards
- Clear, concise language
- Technical accuracy
- Complete information
- Examples and use cases
- Up-to-date content

### Code Documentation
- Comprehensive docstrings
- Type hints everywhere
- Comments explaining why, not what
- API documentation
- Usage examples

### System Documentation
- Architecture overviews
- Component descriptions
- Integration guides
- Configuration instructions
- Troubleshooting guides

### User Documentation
- Clear instructions
- Step-by-step guides
- Screenshots and diagrams
- FAQ sections
- Support information

### Documentation Maintenance
- Regular updates
- Version control
- Change tracking
- Review cycles
- Quality assurance

### Documentation Tools
- Markdown for most content
- YAML for structured data
- Diagrams for visual content
- Code examples in fenced blocks
- Consistent formatting

### Documentation Review
- Peer review process
- Technical accuracy checks
- Clarity and completeness
- User testing
- Regular updates

### Documentation Standards
- Follow established templates
- Use consistent terminology
- Maintain style guides
- Include metadata
- Track changes

